WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.774
ALEX: Like the difference between
texting your smartest friend

00:00:02.774 --> 00:00:05.548
ALEX: versus a hotline that picks
up immediately.

00:00:05.613 --> 00:00:08.311
JAMIE: Except the hotline is also
pretty smart.

00:00:08.376 --> 00:00:10.334
ALEX: Right, the hotline went to a
good school.

00:00:10.399 --> 00:00:12.617
JAMIE: Heh. What about the context
window?

00:00:12.702 --> 00:00:15.517
ALEX: A million tokens. Which is
roughly a whole

00:00:15.517 --> 00:00:18.332
ALEX: novel's worth of text the
model can hold

00:00:18.332 --> 00:00:21.147
ALEX: at once — at the lower price
point.

00:00:21.257 --> 00:00:23.107
RUFUS: That's the genuinely useful
part for power users.

00:00:23.107 --> 00:00:24.958
RUFUS: You can feed it a massive
codebase and

00:00:24.958 --> 00:00:26.808
RUFUS: have it act on the whole
thing, not

00:00:26.808 --> 00:00:28.658
RUFUS: just a chunk.

00:00:28.768 --> 00:00:30.686
JAMIE: I'll give it that. But I
still want

00:00:30.686 --> 00:00:32.604
JAMIE: to know whether "fast"
means anything if the

00:00:32.604 --> 00:00:34.522
JAMIE: reasoning quality drops.
